    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d133">

                <pb facs="0287" n="195" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v01/pg195"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/9394</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Rome</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-04-03T17:00:00+02:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 3, 1918, 5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received April 4, 5.09 a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1497. Baker’s visit to Italy though so hurried has been highly appreciated and
                    will certainly have good effect. When he was first expected at the front the
                    Minister of War accompanied me to meet him and would have done so when he
                    actually came, but he was ill The King being in Rome, as well as all the
                    Ministers, it was necessary for the Secretary to come here. To leave Italy
                    without doing so would have caused great offense. He was met at the station by
                    the Minister of War and Minister of Treasury, besides certain functionaries. The
                    King, the Premier and Minister Nitti all dwelt upon the great moral effect it
                    would have in Italy to send American flag here with small contingent of men. I,
                    myself, believe it to be of the utmost importance to do so, not as a question of
                    increasing the fighting forces here, but because of the moral effect. Italy is
                    looking to America as never before in her history. The Prime Minister <pb
                        facs="0288" n="196" xml:id="pg196"/> said that the plain countrymen of
                    Italy, especially in the south, know little of other countries, but all know of
                    America, and would feel the presence of the flag to be the token that she is
                    fighting for Italy as she is fighting for England and France.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nelson Page</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
